### C. Proactive Migration

RUL and anomaly scores computed at a node enter the source term of the field, so a role is moved off a node at risk of failing before that node stops. In the experiment, RUL_predict is modeled as the true RUL plus Gaussian noise with standard deviation $\sigma$. This is an oracle sensitivity analysis rather than an evaluation of a real AI predictor: it shows how sensitive the migration mechanism is to prediction quality, not that a specific model achieves that quality. Real RUL error is usually non-Gaussian, biased, drifting, and heavy-tailed, so the numbers below should be read as a function of prediction accuracy, and plugging in a real RUL model on public data is the next step. Because migrations run sequentially, they do not contend for spare capacity at the same time as a whole-cluster failure. For $\sigma \le 1.5$ the mechanism keeps almost all roles: 100% at $\sigma \le 1.0$ and 99.9% at $\sigma = 1.5$. The reactive alternative, waiting for the failure and then repairing, keeps about 84% (0.84 to 0.85). The two break even at $\sigma \approx 2.4$; beyond that the prediction is too inaccurate and proactive migration becomes worse than reactive (Fig. 3).

[[FIG:fig5_proactive.png|Fig. 3. RUL-based proactive migration (blue) against the reactive alternative (red). The proactive mechanism keeps close to 100% of roles up to about $\sigma$ = 1.5 and breaks even at $\sigma \approx 2.4$.|470|313]]

### D. Learning Policy: Improvement and Limits

The edge classifier reaches an AUC of 0.94 to 0.95 and nearly saturates at $K=2$ to $3$, showing that only a few hops of information are needed. The routing policy, however, improves little. With learned conductance the field closes only 15 to 34% of the gap to optimum, and in the feasible region typically only 15 to 20%. Once difficulty is controlled, this benefit does not grow with size. An oracle configuration, in which the edges on the optimal max-flow route are amplified by using the correct solution in advance, closes 33 to 100% of the gap. This is an upper bound on what conductance tuning alone can achieve and shows there is still room to research. We also tried learning the router directly with REINFORCE, but it performed worse than the plain field (Fig. 4). The cause is not over-smoothing but the limit of locality: the field already carries a follow-the-current bias that is near-optimal at the neighborhood level, so a GNN that sees only $K$ hops has little residual signal to exploit, and the room the oracle reveals lies in global optimal-route information that a local policy cannot infer from a few hops.

