### E. Control-Plane Partition: Decentralized Against Centralized Recovery

The central question for a decentralized self-healing mechanism is what happens when the central control plane itself disappears. When the control plane is reachable and spare capacity remains, the scheduler of Kubernetes reassigns pods within tens of seconds, so a second controller placed above it produces no measurable difference; the difference appears only when an edge region loses contact with the control plane while its nodes stay alive and can still see each other. To isolate this regime we built a container fabric (Section III-E) in which the partition, node death, and reassignment are all real operations.

The fabric has 15 agents, each an agent holding a set of roles under capacity $b=4$. Three agents are co-located with the control plane (region $s_0$, never partitioned, a control) and twelve form an edge region $s_1$ carrying 36 roles at about 75% of capacity. Roles are local: a role of $s_1$ can only run on an agent in $s_1$, which models edge data locality, so when $s_1$ loses the control plane the control plane cannot move a role to another region. Two virtual networks separate the control channel from the peer channel. A real partition is created by disconnecting all of $s_1$ from the control channel while the agents keep the peer channel, after which $k$ edge nodes are killed. Two recovery modes are compared on exactly the same failure schedule: a baseline that relies only on the central control plane, and CertiHeal, in which the surviving agents in $s_1$ elect a local coordinator, the lowest-index surviving agent, that reassigns orphaned roles over the peer channel, respects the capacity $b$, and needs no control plane. In this degenerate star the certificate reduces to a capacity check: full recovery is feasible if and only if the surviving capacity of the region holds all roles, that is $(12-k)\,b \ge 36$, equivalently $k \le 3$. We sweep $k$ from 1 to 6, five seeds per level, and measure role coverage, the fraction of roles served by at least one live agent.

The results separate cleanly (Table 3, Fig. 5). With the control plane lost, the baseline cannot reassign the orphaned roles in the partitioned region because locality forbids moving them elsewhere: coverage falls linearly with the number of dead nodes, from 92.9% at $k=1$ to 57.1% at $k=6$, and does not recover during the partition. CertiHeal recovers locally over the peer channel and holds exactly 100% coverage across the whole region the certificate declares feasible ($k \le 3$), then tracks the remaining capacity beyond it, at 90.5%, 81.0%, and 71.4% for $k=4, 5, 6$. At every failure level CertiHeal is at least as high as the baseline, and for $k \le 3$ the gap is 7.1 to 21.4 points.

More important than the coverage figure is the relation between the certificate and the outcome. Across all 30 CertiHeal runs (five seeds by six levels), the confusion matrix between "certificate says feasible" and "recovers fully to 100%" is $\text{TP}=15$, $\text{FP}=0$, $\text{TN}=15$, $\text{FN}=0$: whenever the certificate says feasible the system always recovers fully, and whenever the certificate says infeasible the system never recovers fully and never over-promises. The false-safe rate, the probability of a hung role when the certificate declares success, is zero. This is a direct realization of the fail-closed property that Section IV-F states as the operational target: when the real constraints match the capacitated flow model, the model certificate becomes an operational commitment that does not fail. The recovery time of CertiHeal grows with the number of roles to reassign, from about 6 s at $k=1$ to about 27 s at $k=6$ (Fig. 5), the coordination time of a paced local-coordinator loop.

The measured coverage is deterministic across all five seeds, with no variance: the baseline loses exactly $3k$ roles and does not recover, while CertiHeal recovers to the capacity ceiling of the region. This fabric is a real container implementation of decentralized recovery, but it is a separate harness rather than Kubernetes; Section IV-F adds the validation on Kubernetes itself.

