### F. Validation on a Real Kubernetes Cluster: Operational Certificate, Split-Brain, and Fencing

The fabric of Section IV-E isolates the decentralized mechanism but runs on a separate harness. Two quantities appear only on a real orchestrator: the accuracy of the certificate under scheduling noise, and split-brain behavior under partition. We deploy the mechanism on a real k3d/K3s cluster (Section III-E), extended to a 16-node fabric across two physical machines connected over the internet by a mesh VPN (Tailscale, WAN latency 70 to 90 ms; three nodes on the first machine, thirteen on the second), running 64 roles.

On this platform we inject a chaos schedule of 180 random node kills over three hours, generated in sync on both machines from a shared seed: every 45 to 150 s one to three nodes are killed, occasionally up to seven at once, rejoining after 30 to 180 s. Three hours of continuous, random, and overlapping failure on real distributed infrastructure show that the mechanism operates at this scale and duration: the system is at full 64 of 64 roles for 93.6% of the time, with a median reassignment time of about 2 s that is nearly independent of the number of simultaneous failures (detection time remains bounded by the centralized node-monitor-grace-period of Kubernetes). The most valuable quantity here is the certificate, which no Kubernetes configuration emits: it predicts 96.2% of the events, in that no role hangs when the number of failed nodes does not exceed the certificate, and roles do become unplaceable when it does, up to 31 at the peak, matching the lower-bound roles of the certificate (Fig. 6).

Two layers must be separated, and the two experiments complement each other. $C(F,S)$ is the model certificate, exact in the modeled capacitated flow network; the controlled fabric of Section IV-E, where every assignment is accepted exactly by real capacity, gives no counterexample and a false-safe rate of zero. On the real K3s cluster, unmodeled constraints such as scheduling order and timing lower its accuracy as an operational predictor to 96.2%: the remaining 3.8% are cases where a role hangs although the certificate said it would not, even though the graph theorem still holds. For a system that calls itself certified, the most dangerous number is not the overall accuracy but the probability of a hang when the certificate declares success, the false-safe rate, which is quite different from a conservative false negative. The two numbers mark the upgrade path: the model certificate is exact, and turning it into an operational commitment that does not fail on noisy infrastructure requires a fail-closed step, issuing the certificate only after a real constraint check accepts the whole assignment, a dry-run or admission on Kubernetes or an actual reservation, at the cost of a small verification delay. The fabric experiment of Section IV-E is exactly the realization of that fail-closed branch as a capacity-checked reservation, and there the false-safe rate is zero. In this paper, certified refers to the model certificate that has been proved; the path to a full operational commitment is the fail-closed step just described.

A real wide-area partition also exposes a safety problem that stopping a container does not show. When the second machine is disconnected, its edge nodes stay alive and keep running their old roles while the control plane creates replacements, so during the partition window the number of concurrently running replicas doubles; for a stateful service, two writing replicas can corrupt data. We add a lease-based fencing layer: a node that has lost contact with the control plane beyond a lease threshold freezes itself and stops serving before its replacement takes over. Measured directly on the same partition, without fencing the isolated side serves throughout the partition (41 roles alongside 18 replacements), the concurrent-execution window lasts the full 161 s and is in principle unbounded in the length of the outage; with fencing the isolated side stops after the lease expires, and the window shrinks to about 49 s and then to zero for the rest of the partition (Fig. 7). The layer trades a short pause bounded by the lease threshold for a determinate upper bound on concurrent-execution exposure, in place of a window that grows with the outage.

